      "exercise_text_sha256": "c61f85ba79b219f8d6ebee9cee9d4f06febc92c2d47b53c79253a45a498730bd",
      "source_text": "(a) Let $\\tilde\\pi$ be feasible, $J_c(\\tilde\\pi)\\le d$. Then $-\\lambda(J_c(\\tilde\\pi)-d)\\ge0$ because $\\lambda\\ge0$ and $J_c(\\tilde\\pi)-d\\le0$, so $L(\\tilde\\pi,\\lambda)\\ge J_r(\\tilde\\pi)$. Hence $D(\\lambda)=\\sup_\\pi L(\\pi,\\lambda)\\ge L(\\tilde\\pi,\\lambda)\\ge J_r(\\tilde\\pi)$. Taking the supremum over feasible $\\tilde\\pi$ gives $D(\\lambda)\\ge p^\\star$. No property of $\\Pi$ was used, which is why the bound holds for neural-network policy classes.",
